# Hostile audit — contact-chain safe-area and lattice-capacity theorem

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Audited source:** `research/target_c_contact_chain_safe_area_capacity.md`  
**Verdict:** PASS at the stated proof-only scope.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

## 1. Incremental constant

For unit disks centered one unit apart, the full overlap lens has area

\[
 L=2\pi/3-\sqrt3/2.
\]

The edge line bisects that lens. A half-disk has area `pi/2`, so the one-sided part of
the new disk not already covered by the preceding disk is

\[
 c_*=\pi/2-L/2
 =\pi/6+\sqrt3/4.
\]

The complementary constant is

\[
 h_*=\pi/2-c_*=\pi/3-\sqrt3/4.
\]

These identities are exact.

## 2. Convex-chain union bound

Order a convex unit-contact boundary path as `p_0,...,p_ell`. For each `i>=1`, the
whole convex polygon lies in the interior half-plane of the preceding boundary edge
`[p_{i-1},p_i]`. The portion of disk `B(p_i,1)` which was not covered by **all**
earlier disks is a subset of the portion not covered by the immediately preceding
disk. It is therefore at most `c_*` in area.

The initial disk is contained in the interior half-plane of the first boundary edge,
so it contributes at most `pi/2`.

This argument does not assume that nonconsecutive disks are disjoint; extra overlap
only strengthens the upper bound. It also does not assume a small-turn boundary.

**Verdict:** Theorem 2.1 is valid.

## 3. Internal safe area

A convex internal contact face with `m` boundary vertices is handled by opening its
cycle at one vertex and using the path through all `m` vertices. The path has `m-1`
unit edges, so the entire forbidden union of boundary-vertex unit disks has area at
most

\[
 \pi/2+(m-1)c_*=mc_*+h_*.
\]

The safe region differs from the complement of the closed disk union only on
measure-zero circles/boundary pieces, so the area inequality is unaffected.

**Verdict:** the internal bound is valid.

## 4. Exterior safe area

For a boundary closing chord of length `s`, the unit neighborhood on the interior side
of the supporting line is a rectangle of area `s` plus two quarter-disks, hence area
`s+pi/2`.

This neighborhood contains the full unit disks centered at both chord endpoints.
Therefore only the `ell-1` internal vertices of the complementary `ell`-edge contact
path need be added. Adding them in path order costs at most `(ell-1)c_*` by the same
half-plane argument. Hence total forbidden area is at most

\[
 s+\pi/2+(\ell-1)c_*=s+\ell c_*+h_*.
\]

No assumption about the chord being a contact is made.

**Verdict:** the exterior bound is valid.

## 5. Lattice realization

The unit triangular lattice has covolume `a_0=sqrt(3)/2`. Translation averaging of
the integer-valued count in a bounded measurable region gives mean `area/a_0`, hence
some translate has at least its floor. The selected lattice set is unit-separated.

The companion wall-shield theorems then provide separation from retained old source
points, so the lattice points are actual replacement points rather than a density
heuristic.

**Verdict:** the capacity lower bound is constructive.

## 6. Constant algebra

For the internal residual

\[
 r_\partial=A/a_0-m/2+1-z,
\]

one has `A/a_0=z+r_partial+m/2-1`. Also

\[
 {c_*\over a_0}=1/2+{\pi\over3\sqrt3},
\qquad
 {h_*\over a_0}={2\pi\over3\sqrt3}-1/2.
\]

Therefore

\[
 {area(S_\partial)\over a_0}
 \ge z+r_\partial-{\pi\over3\sqrt3}m
 -\left({1\over2}+{2\pi\over3\sqrt3}\right),
\]

which gives the printed internal floor bound exactly.

For an exterior cap, canonical graph-boundary count is `k_C=ell+1`: the `ell` old
contact edges plus the one added closing edge form a simple augmented polygon. Thus

\[
 r_C=\sigma_C-k_C=A/a_0+s/2-\ell/2-z,
\]

and

\[
 A/a_0=z+r_C+\ell/2-s/2.
\]

Subtracting the safe-area loss `s+ell c_*+h_*` divided by `a_0` gives exactly

\[
 z+r_C-{\pi\over3\sqrt3}\ell
 -\left({2\over\sqrt3}+{1\over2}\right)s
 -{h_*\over a_0}.
\]

The closure thresholds follow because `tau(H)` is an integer and
`floor(X)>=tau(H)` whenever `X>=tau(H)`.

**Verdict:** all coefficients and floors are correct.

## 7. Scope and limitation

The theorem does not show that a selected Type-I chamber has residual above the
printed threshold. It only converts sufficiently large residual into realized packing
slots with an exact coefficient.

Residual conservation still gives only `sum r_j=R_F`; it does not force the
macroscopic chamber to own a fixed fraction of `R_F`.

No Oler inequality is reversed, no KKT coefficient floor is assumed, no source point
is lost, and no contact isolate enters the Section-57 affine kernel.

**Final verdict:** PASS. This is genuine Type-I progress: the former qualitative
capacity realization gap now has an explicit finite sufficient condition with a
universal contact-chain coefficient. C-II remains open.
